To fix this issue, follow the correct usage order and ensure all parameters comply with the defined constraints. <br><br><b>Allowed Algorithms:</b><br><br><code>SHA256withRSA</code>, <code>SHA256withECDSA</code>, <code>SHA256withDSA</code>, <code>SHA384withRSA</code>, <code>SHA512withRSA</code>, <code>SHA384withECDSA</code>, <code>SHA512withECDSA</code><br><br><b>Correct Order of Operations:</b><ul><li><code>getInstance()</code></li><li><code>initSign()</code> or <code>initVerify()</code></li><li><code>update()</code></li><li><code>sign()</code> or <code>verify()</code></li></ul><br><b>Byte Array Constraints:</b><ul><li><code>offset >= 0</code></li><li><code>len > 0</code></li><li><code>input.length >= offset + len</code></li></ul>These ensure safe array access during signature operations.<br><br><b>Signing Example:</b><br><pre><code>import java.security.*;

public class SignExample {
 public byte[] signData(byte[] input, PrivateKey privateKey) throws Exception {
 Signature signature = Signature.getInstance("SHA256withRSA");
        signature.initSign(privateKey);
 signature.update(input, 0, input.length);  // offset = 0, len = input.length
 return signature.sign();
    }
}</code></pre><br><b>Verification Example:</b><br><pre><code>import java.security.*;

public class VerifyExample {
 public boolean verifySignature(byte[] input, byte[] sigBytes, PublicKey publicKey) throws Exception {
 Signature signature = Signature.getInstance("SHA256withRSA");
  signature.initVerify(publicKey);
 signature.update(input, 0, input.length);
 return signature.verify(sigBytes);
}
}</code></pre><br><b>Avoid:</b><ul><li>Using <code>offset</code> or <code>len</code> values that exceed array bounds</li><li>Skipping init methods before calling <code>update()</code></li><li>Using weak or unlisted algorithms</li></ul>

